"[2511.05852] Can Fine-Tuning Erase Your Edits? On the Fragile Coexistence of Knowledge Editing and Adaptation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.05852)\nciteturn2587view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.05852\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 8 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 27 Jun 2026 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:Can Fine-Tuning Erase Your Edits? On the Fragile Coexistence of Knowledge Editing and Adaptation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yinjie Cheng , cite7†Paul Youssef , cite8†Christin Seifert , cite9†Jörg Schlötterer , cite10†Zhixue Zhao L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Can Fine-Tuning Erase Your Edits? On the Fragile Coexistence of Knowledge Editing and Adaptation, by Yinjie Cheng and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Knowledge editing (KE) offers a lightweight alternative to retraining for updating large language models (LLMs). Meanwhile, fine-tuning remains the default operation for adapting LLMs to new domains and tasks.\nL17: Despite their widespread adoption, these two post-training interventions have been studied in isolation, leaving open a crucial question: if we fine-tune an edited model, do the edits survive? This question is motivated by practical objectives: removing covert or malicious edits, and preserving beneficial edits.\nL18: If fine-tuning impairs edits (Fig.1), current KE methods become less efficient, as a newly fine-tuned model requires re-editing; if edits persist, fine-tuned models risk propagating hidden malicious edits, raising serious safety concerns. To this end, we systematically quantify edit decay after fine-tuning across 254 experimental configurations. Our results show that in general, edits decay substantially after subsequent fine-tuning.\nL19: AlphaEdit exhibits the greatest decay on the zsRE benchmark when applied to GPT-J, where 25.27% of previously successful edits become unsuccessful after fine-tuning. We further find that fine-tuning only the edited layers is sufficient to effectively remove edits, while incurring only modest degradation in downstream performance. Surprisingly, fine-tuning non-edited layers leads to greater edit decay than all-layer fine-tuning.\nL20: Besides, our activation space analysis reveals that fine-tuning produces a larger and more coherent representational shift, both in magnitude and direction, than KE. Overall, our study underscores the necessity of evaluating KE within the broader LLM application pipeline.\nL21: Comments:  | Accepted to KDD 2026\nL22: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL23: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.2.7\nL24: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.05852 [cs.CL]\nL25:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.05852v4 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL26:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.05852†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL27: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite16†https://doi.org/10.1145/3770855.3817879†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL28: ## Submission history\nL29: \nL30: From: Yinjie Cheng [cite17†view email ]\nL31: cite5†[v1] Sat, 8 Nov 2025 04:58:03 UTC (3,869 KB)\nL32: cite18†[v2] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 09:17:35 UTC (1 KB) (withdrawn)\nL33: cite19†[v3] Sun, 7 Dec 2025 19:23:41 UTC (4,660 KB)\nL34: [v4] Sat, 27 Jun 2026 23:13:01 UTC (6,555 KB)\nL35: \nL36: Full-text links:\nL37: ## Access Paper:\nL38: \nL39: View a PDF of the paper titled Can Fine-Tuning Erase Your Edits? On the Fragile Coexistence of Knowledge Editing 